= Property =

== Overview ==
A '''property''' is a characteristic, quality, attribute or formally represented predicate that can be attributed to something.

Examples:
* red;
* has a mass of 2 kg;
* was created in 2026;
* has author Alice.

The word is used differently in philosophy, logic, programming, databases and Semantic Web technology.

== Philosophical properties ==
In metaphysics, properties are often treated as repeatable features things can exemplify.

Examples include:
* redness;
* mass;
* shape;
* being prime.

Philosophers disagree about whether properties are universals, tropes, sets, predicates or something else.

The ontology of properties is therefore contested.

== Predicate and property ==
A predicate is a linguistic or formal expression.

A property is what the predicate may represent or attribute.

For example, the predicate:

<pre>
isRed(x)
</pre>

can express the property of being red.

But one property can be expressed by different predicates, and predicates can fail to correspond neatly to real properties.

== Property and relation ==
A one-place property can be represented as a unary predicate.

A multi-place [[Relation|relation]] connects several relata.

Some frameworks treat properties as relations of particular arity.

Others distinguish qualities from relations.

The terminology should be interpreted within the chosen formalism.

== Attributes in data systems ==
Data systems often use "attribute", "field" or "property" for named values associated with a record.

Example:

<pre>
Person:
  name = Alice
  birthDate = ...
</pre>

This practical usage need not imply a philosophical commitment about properties.

== RDF properties ==
In RDF, a property is a resource used as the predicate of triples.

Example:

<pre>
Alice  knows  Bob
</pre>

The RDF property <code>knows</code> represents a binary relation from subject to object.

RDF properties are first-class resources and can themselves have labels, descriptions and hierarchy.

== RDFS subproperties ==
RDF Schema allows:

<pre>
p1 rdfs:subPropertyOf p2
</pre>

If:

<pre>
x p1 y
</pre>

then RDFS semantics entails:

<pre>
x p2 y
</pre>

Subproperty semantics is therefore stronger than merely grouping similarly named fields.

== Domain and range ==
RDFS can state:

<pre>
p rdfs:domain C
p rdfs:range D
</pre>

These declarations imply type information when p is used.

They are not conventional closed-world validation constraints.

This is a common source of modeling errors.

== OWL object and data properties ==
OWL distinguishes:

'''Object properties''' — relate individuals to individuals.

'''Data properties''' — relate individuals to data values.

OWL can add characteristics such as:
* functionality;
* transitivity;
* symmetry;
* inverse relationships.

These are logical semantics, not UI metadata.

== Property values ==
A property type and a property assertion should be distinguished.

<pre>
property type: birthDate
assertion: Alice birthDate 2000-01-01
</pre>

The first defines/references a relation.

The second claims that the relation holds for a particular subject and value.

== Metadata properties ==
[[Metadata]] systems use properties such as:
* title;
* creator;
* date;
* license;
* subject.

Dublin Core provides widely reused metadata properties.

Using shared property identifiers can improve interoperability.

== BITwiki application ==
BITwiki defines local semantic properties such as Domain, Entity type, Depends on and Provenance.

They are dimensions of BITwiki's representation, not the universal set of properties reality possesses.

== Epistemic notes ==
Encoding a property assertion makes a claim. Its truth and evidence remain separate questions from whether the property is syntactically valid.

